{"name":"Lady Anne","hex":"#fde2de","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"lady","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The mistress of a household.","A woman of breeding or higher class, a woman of authority."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To address as “lady”."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lady"}],"entries":[{"title":"Anne Bacon","blocks":[{"text":"Anne, Lady Bacon was an English lady and scholar. She made a lasting contribution to English religious literature with her translation from Latin of John Jewel's Apologie of the Anglican Church (1564). She was the mother of Francis Bacon."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/AnneCookeBacon.jpg/330px-AnneCookeBacon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anne_Bacon","wikidata":"Q475067"},{"title":"Lady Anne Barnard","blocks":[{"text":"Lady Anne Barnard was a Scottish travel writer, artist and socialite, and the author of the ballad Auld Robin Gray. Her five-year residence in Cape Town, South Africa, although brief, had a significant impact on the cultural and social life of the time."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c5/LadyAnneBarnardPortrait.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lady_Anne_Barnard","wikidata":"Q3510181"},{"title":"Lady","blocks":[{"text":"A lady is a woman who behaves in a polite way. Once used to describe only women of a high social class or status, the female counterpart of lord, now it may refer to any adult woman, as gentleman can be used for men."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lady","wikidata":"Q1378024"},{"title":"Anne","blocks":[{"text":"Anne, alternatively spelled Ann, is a form of the Latin female name Anna. This in turn is a representation of the Hebrew Hannah, which means 'favour' or 'grace'. Related names include Annie and Ana.